{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 10, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the origins of facebook with precision, intensity, and emotional depth. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie transcends the typical biopic genre by transforming a story about code and connectivity into a gripping tale of ambition, betrayal, and isolation.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him not as a villain or hero, but as a brilliant, socially awkward prodigy whose genius is matched only by his emotional detachment. his performance is sharp, controlled, and unnervingly authentic, capturing the quiet arrogance and simmering resentment that fuel zuckerberg’s rise. justin timberlake, as sean parker, brings electric charisma and chaotic energy, embodying the seductive allure of tech utopianism and its dangerous consequences. andrew garfield and jesse plemons round out the core cast with nuanced portrayals of the winklevoss twins and erik, respectively, each conveying complex emotions beneath layers of privilege and betrayal.\n\nsorkin’s screenplay is a triumph of dialogue — rapid-fire, layered, and laced with wit and irony. every line feels deliberate, every exchange a chess move in a game of power and perception. the non-linear structure, weaving courtroom depositions with flashbacks, adds narrative tension and deepens the moral ambiguity of the story. we’re never quite sure who to root for, and that’s the point.\n\nfincher’s direction is immaculate. the cinematography is cold and sleek, mirroring the sterile world of silicon valley, while trent reznor and atticus ross’s haunting, electronic score pulses like the heartbeat of the digital age — minimalist, urgent, and deeply unsettling. the pacing never falters; even scenes of legal proceedings feel like high-stakes showdowns.\n\nbeneath the surface,